The rise of rock and roll in the 1950s was significantly influenced by the earlier rhythm and blues genre."}], "places": [{"name": "Detroit, USA", "role": "Major center of R&B"}, {"name": "Memphis, USA", "role": "Cultural hub for R&B"}, {"name": "Chicago, USA", "role": "Birthplace of urban blues"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: rhythm and blues", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "rhythm-and-blues"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q45981", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhythm_and_blues"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "rhythm and blues left a notable mark on Modern history, documented across 84 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Rhythm and blues significantly shaped the trajectory of American music and culture, influencing the emergence of rock and roll and serving as a voice for social change during the civil rights movement.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-09T01:26:08Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27177785068", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Genre of popular music that originated within african-american communities in the 1940s Created c. 1938.", "newValue": "Rhythm and blues (R&B) emerged as a pivotal genre of popular music during the 1940s, originating primarily within African-American communities in the United States. The genre's roots can be traced back to earlier forms of African-American music, including blues, jazz, and gospel, which collectively laid the groundwork for a new musical expression that resonated with the struggles and aspirations of black Americans. Key figures in the development of rhythm and blues include influential artists such as Ray Charles and Ruth Brown, who not only defined the sound of the genre but also paved the way for future musical innovations.

The genre was characterized by its strong backbeat, soulful vocals, and emotive lyrics, often addressing themes of love, heartbreak, and social issues. In 1949, the term 'rhythm and blues' was officially adopted by Billboard magazine to replace the label 'Harlem Hit Parade.' The 1950s saw the rise of R&B as a commercial force, with hits like 'Shake, Rattle and Roll' by Big Joe Turner and 'The Great Pretender' by The Platters achieving widespread popularity. This period also witnessed the integration of R&B into mainstream music, influencing the development of rock and roll, which subsequently dominated the music scene in the 1960s.

